Scheme syntax:
sns:<address>
address := [ <mailbox> "@" ] <name> "." <tld>
Colon-only form; producers MUST NOT emit "sns://" (per ODNCA-STD-005;
consumers MAY accept and strip a stray "//" on input). <name>.<tld> is
an SNS name per ODNCA-STD-003: UTF-8, exactly one dot, no whitespace or
control characters. No DNS interaction at any point.

Scheme semantics: per ODNCA-STD-005 §2 — the URI names the identifier;
the action is taken from context by the handling application (payment
to the name's holder, or mail to the mailbox), never executed without
explicit user confirmation.

Encoding considerations: UTF-8 names; percent-encoding per RFC 3986.
No IDNA/punycode processing.

Interoperability considerations: no DNS resolution; unaware
applications treat it as an unknown scheme per RFC 3986. No authority
component, no default port.

Security considerations: dereferencing resolves a mutable
name-to-holder binding; clients MUST display the resolved address
before any signing operation and SHOULD verify the binding against a
committed on-chain state rather than trust a single index. Handlers
MUST NOT auto-sign: a "sns" URI is an intent, not an authorization.
Visually confusable Unicode names carry the usual homograph risks.
